Z||000005||Will||12-18-2001||10:39 PM||||I have a question:<BR>Exactly which radio is the UP3?<BR>I just got a radio from a '97 Grand Am. It has a single CD slot with the 7 band EQ. Is that the UP3?<P>I guess the theftlock procedure would be the same, but what I really want to know is: where can I find wiring diagrams to make a wiring adapter, or purchase a ready-made adapter, for that matter?<P>Does the modern radio work with the Fiero factory sub?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Devotshka||12-18-2001||10:53 PM||||This is the UP3 radio:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.aatechnologies.com/cmyradios/gifs/Pont_up3.GIF">  <P>TheftLock is for the most part the same on all i've seen, but i haven't had to mess with any newer than 97... i know the 2000-up Bonneville radios changed quite a bit if i recall, so newer models may be different.<P>Most car audio installation shops can sell you an adaptor (often an actual cars harness cut off, depending on where you go) to plug into your radio for $5-20 depending on if its a new part or not.  Then you buy an aftermarket adaptor for your factory harness and wire the two together.  The aftermarket adaptor can be gotten from Walmart, CircuitCity, etc for considerably less than an audio shop will charge you.<P>As for getting the factory sub to get along with a newer radio, i *believe* you have to do a minor modification to the harness, but i'm not sure...someone else should have some input that, i believe its been discussed here before.<P>&lt;edit&gt; didn't mean to quote &lt;/edit&gt;<p>[This message has been edited by Devotshka (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||12-19-2001||12:44 AM||||Yes, that's the one.<BR>Can it also control a CD changer? It looks like it has a lot of pins to plug into...<P>So the adapter I buy from the audio shop plugs into the back of my radio and adapts it to what, exactly? An industry standard pinout?<P>Then this other adapter to buy from Wally World plugs into the car's harness and adapts it to what? The same industry standard pinout?<P>Basically, what exactly do I ask for in the audio shop or the Walmart?<P>Thanks<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Devotshka||12-19-2001||12:55 AM||||The factory plug from the audio shop is a GM style plug, it plugs into your new radio (bring your new radio in with you) and has loose wires hanging off the back of it.<BR>The aftermarket radio adaptor (i think its listed as General Motors 1980-1989) is a socket for your existing wiring harness to plug into, and has loose wires hanging off the back. <BR>You hook the wires from each harness together (the aftermarket adaptor will have a color coded diagram).<BR>As for the cd changer controller, i'm not sure if that option was available in combination with that radio.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||12-19-2001||12:58 AM||||Cool. Sounds like a project for the next day or two.||||reg||1
Z||000010||tgowens||12-19-2001||02:48 AM||||The adapter can be bought at a GM dealer for around $18.50 - it adapts the older style (pre-a989) 12 pin plug to the newer style 21 pin plug and also comes with the antenna adapter (the newer radios use a smaller antenna lead-in.<P>There is a small mod to the wiring if you are using the factory sub -- you need to clip the two pink wires from the sub amp and splice them into the yellow(hot in accessory) wire from the car harness - that's all there is to it......<P>Oh, I almost forgot -- you will need to break out the triangular mount in the rear of the radio housing in the console. It is held to the plastic console frame with two rivits. Pry it out with a large flat blade screwdriver. The reason for this is because the earlier radio plug fit vertically, the plug on the UP3 fits horizontally - if you try to mash it back in there with that bracket still in place it can cause one side of the 21 pin plug to pop out and you will have no power on the radio.<P>By the way, on the adaptor I mentioned, there are two plugs not used (Fieros use only the black and blue plugs into the radio and the two wire power plug - the other plugs are for Chrysler applications.<P>Also, the UP3 sits further back into the radio housing so if you want to make it flush with the trim bezel you will need to put small shims behind the 7mm screws that hold the radio in the console support. I use the thin spark plug wire separators.......<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||12-19-2001||03:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>The adapter can be bought at a GM dealer for around $18.50 - it adapts the older style (pre-a989) 12 pin plug to the newer style 21 pin plug and also comes with the antenna adapter (the newer radios use a smaller antenna lead-in.<BR>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-19-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do you know the part number?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mickey_Moose||12-19-2001||06:47 PM||||Thanks for the info - now I can set the lock on the radio one day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Tim<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000013||WampusCat||12-19-2001||11:03 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> Do you know the part number?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>GM part # 16140051 <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013963.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013963.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Will||12-20-2001||03:29 PM||||What exactly is TheftLock? I plugged mine in and it fired right up. I guess it didn't have theftloc enabled.<P>I used an aftermarket adapter from Metra Electronics (www.metraonline.com). I don't know the Metra part number.<BR>I did the splicing on the adapter, not the car harness itself.<P>Only the upper left and lower right mounting holes line up. I put 1/4" of washers under each screw to get the right spacing.<P>I didn't like the idea of breaking out the rear support and having no rear support, so I tore the entire dash and console apart to be able to get to the rear support and notch it to clear the plugs. I was going to have to tear the dash and console apart for engine swap wiring anyway.<P>It has a few typical used radio problems, like a scratchy volume control, but that should disappear with use.<P>Thanks for all the info.||||reg||1
